#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "f888277f-085b-5c6a-b50c-8ffd9095bc53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2327" :question/problem "In a garden, there are $40$ flowers, $30 \\%$ of which are roses. How many roses are there in the garden?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%,$ and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 40 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 40 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{40}{10} \\\\  & =3\\times 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/id #uuid "03c003db-96d2-5588-a1fa-944b9305cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2329" :question/problem "Dora works $800$ hours per year. She rests for $12.5 \\%$ ​​of her total working hours. How much time does Dora rest per year at work?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%,$ and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =0.125\\times 800 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5}{100}\\times 800 \\\\  & =12.5\\times \\frac{800}{100} \\\\  & =12.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/id #uuid "f409e34f-c8f7-5e21-b024-a0c7012c496d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2330" :question/problem "Marcus has a tank with a capacity of $1,500$ liters. He decides to buy a second tank whose capacity is $130 \\%$ of the capacity of the first tank. What is the capacity of the second tank?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1.3\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =130\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =130\\times 15 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a93fb62-2ec9-565e-9c57-f9c082c80ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "382\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442041} {:answer/id #uuid "6006ad1e-1a37-5be5-99a2-aad31c2e4c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442044} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442059} {:answer/id #uuid "a06496e3-b1f9-5236-81ae-640526c84374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442062} {:answer/id #uuid "b0df4d7e-6c40-5596-8934-d5200922b5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "367\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab69ccc-fa6f-5437-b26e-0cfa7c46334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442059} :answer-field/id #uuid "2be4b384-c722-53de-acff-4d66797cbf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e9d26ed-5508-5ed1-9b21-0aeebb8bd487" :question/math-academy-id "q-169405" :question/problem "A dough weighs $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ Of this amount, $24.5%$ represents the weight of wheat flour in the dough. How much wheat flour is in the dough?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the dough, which is $1,400\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $24.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n24.5%=24.5÷100=0.245\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =0.245\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n24.5%=\\frac{24.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =24.5%\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =\\frac{24.5}{100}\\times 1,400 \\\\  & =24.5\\times \\frac{1,400}{100} \\\\  & =24.5\\times 14 \\\\  & =343.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $24.5%$ of $1,400$ is $343.$ Thus, there are $343\\,\\text{g}$ of wheat flour in the dough."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442031} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4efd2-95f6-53a5-990f-05462c227746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442037} {:answer/id #uuid "50285ed8-6ea0-5ff3-9202-35daf33c5c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442039} {:answer/id #uuid "90e6a243-58b0-5ce5-b09f-0c27c5bb5725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442055} {:answer/id #uuid "d0389f3d-c421-56fc-bba0-72d413f16b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "332a7e28-20ac-51e5-b852-2d91c5421572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442031} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ad4ce9-3fce-5321-afb5-03c2b2f94153" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7304750-8de2-5c9e-abd7-f379f2372291" :question/math-academy-id "q-169407" :question/problem "In January $2022,$ a social media account had approximately $53,000$ followers. In December of that year, the account had $120%$ the number of followers it had in January. Determine the number of followers the account had in December, $2022.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of followers the account had in January, $2022,$ which is $53,000.$ The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of followers the account had in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 53,000 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{53,000}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 10,600 \\\\  & =63,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $53,000$ is $63,600.$ So, the social media account had $63,600$ followers in December $2022.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442053} {:answer/id #uuid "959684e0-6160-5bc3-ae20-254a6b93f357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442058} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c3a3da-c289-5d72-94a6-ce1ccba391f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442064} {:answer/id #uuid "ac816f30-ec56-5811-83e0-1d3c797b5479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442067} {:answer/id #uuid "b131c800-4d6f-5e41-af05-8e80beb85a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d47578-6ae9-50a7-80e8-49a6ec319a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442053} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89da194-a3c1-561d-9eff-8a2b3e5c9607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b046c04a-6183-5340-90ee-d461156c145c" :question/math-academy-id "q-60944" :question/problem "$50%$ of $24$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes. How many students have brown eyes?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of students in Joan's class, which is $24.$ The percentage is $50%,$ and the part is the number of students that have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n50%=50÷100=0.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.5\\times 24 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n50%=\\frac{50}{100}=\\frac{50÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =50%\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $50%$ of $24$ is $12.$ So, $12$ students in Joan's class have brown eyes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfaf78ba-56ea-5dbc-bf0f-d1fb29590063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144855} {:answer/id #uuid "40134deb-7389-5d68-97b3-20daad4c3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144856} {:answer/id #uuid "4177f036-3101-5274-94c5-0432db971310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144857} {:answer/id #uuid "49127b0f-1888-5371-bc26-5a32fc6aa407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144858} {:answer/id #uuid "6165c10f-dd7b-5cc9-a196-5ed39588b90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4177f036-3101-5274-94c5-0432db971310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144857} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f38b3d-16bd-57d3-bbbf-61126981b5ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12ea6424-b692-5183-972b-43b6ca16c730" :question/math-academy-id "q-60947" :question/problem "The original price of a toy robot in a department store was $$75.$ The store manager decides to reduce the price by $12%.$ How much of a discount has the manager given?" :question/worked-solution "To find the discount, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the old price, which is $$75.$ The percentage is $12%,$ and the part is the discount in dollars.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =0.12\\times 75 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12%=\\frac{12}{100}=\\frac{12÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\times 75 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{75}{25} \\\\  & =3\\times 3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04be5953-70b3-58ac-84de-7aec8f