o a fraction:\n\n$$\n110%=\\frac{110}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =110%\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 12,000 \\\\  & =110\\times \\frac{12,000}{100} \\\\  & =110\\times 120 \\\\  & =13,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $110%$ of $12,000$ is $13,200.$ So, Elena earned $$13,200$ in $2019.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5ff36f-e491-5fbc-be2a-d05a4910c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144993} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e2438b-8804-5100-a33f-9ff965f57669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144994} {:answer/id #uuid "53078489-7d20-5c4e-a42f-9ebece7ea273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144995} {:answer/id #uuid "5420f63d-b66a-5b90-9e6e-d2f1e7ef6df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144996} {:answer/id #uuid "72111a34-8816-5e71-b807-150a1f69c95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72111a34-8816-5e71-b807-150a1f69c95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ goals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144997} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e641c71-1959-5139-a611-ce8b45e70ddb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10ea9fad-f3a1-5e56-b19e-836f2905a77a" :question/math-academy-id "q-61189" :question/problem "In his first season in the youth soccer league, Fernando scored $8$ goals. In his second season, he scored $250%$ of the number of goals he made in the first season. How many goals did Fernando score in his second season?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of goals Fernando scored in the first season, which is $8$ goals. The percentage is $250%,$ and the part is the number of goals Fernando scored in the second season.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250%=250÷100=2.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =2.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=\\frac{250÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442035} {:answer/id #uuid "9382fc3e-b6d4-5d3b-afcd-d5ef2ff80f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442057} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4740c7-8d67-5aa0-96ae-bf7491594956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442060} {:answer/id #uuid "cd328c7d-a730-5ea3-b0b3-e6db72ad6ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442077} {:answer/id #uuid "f4672c57-6581-5bf1-8f3d-11582259c677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.5$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ae97699-b5d7-5028-930f-7b9665c19d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ bulbs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442035} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0bbe1a-ad55-5803-8aad-4b37d37084df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d438f5d5-3cb2-537d-8c22-588564d642dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93016" :question/problem "A machine produces $200$ light bulbs per day. Of this amount, $1.5%$ of the bulbs are defective. How many bulbs made each day are defective?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of bulbs produced, which is $200.$ The percentage is $1.5%,$ and the part is the number of defective bulbs.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n1.5%=1.5÷100=0.015\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.015\\times 200 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n1.5%=\\frac{1.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =1.5%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{100}\\times 200 \\\\  & =1.5\\times \\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =1.5\\times 2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1.5%$ of $200$ is $3.$ Thus, $3$ defective bulbs are produced each day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a83b6b0-9ea3-5004-955c-fd0404996dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2,547$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442029} {:answer/id #uuid "733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2,457$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442050} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd81fc7-b319-5dae-b254-133516190a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4,752$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442054} {:answer/id #uuid "a5893e8b-a93c-5af4-b133-8ca8b7b40265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5,472$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442066} {:answer/id #uuid "e18b17dc-80dc-58e9-846a-53998f339a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4,257$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733d17b7-acfa-5ad5-ad56-a38624e442bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2,457$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442050} :answer-field/id #uuid "00053683-f910-5b7b-b30c-4f5366d1b383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53cc30e1-a9ba-5d93-9a6b-7ee8476d4881" :question/math-academy-id "q-93049" :question/problem "Emma earns $$19,500$ a year. Of this amount, she must pay $12.6%$ in taxes. How much money should Emma pay in taxes?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Emma earns annually, which is $$19,500.$ The percentage is $12.6%,$ and the part is the amount she must pay in taxes.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.6%=12.6÷100=0.126\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =0.126\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.6%=\\frac{12.6}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.6%\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.6}{100}\\times 19,500 \\\\  & =12.6\\times \\frac{19,500}{100} \\\\  & =12.6\\times 195 \\\\  & =2,457\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.6%$ of $19,500$ is $2,457.$ Hence, Emma must pay $$2,457$ in taxes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$96$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442025} {:answer/id #uuid "4970ee8f-74a5-5f59-8b2c-a3b5b27799a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$100$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442038} {:answer/id #uuid "6c91863b-dbc4-5d16-880b-03f1f4c8ba25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$104$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442048} {:answer/id #uuid "b24c08f0-03b4-5856-890f-04609b9bb16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$90$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442073} {:answer/id #uuid "f30f15c8-5fe3-59a3-bbe3-e8a6af56f137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$92$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7257d4-5148-5d0f-8b43-54dc6cdb87c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$96$ customers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442025} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e42af57-2ee7-5552-bc66-54d7d4b4f912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ab23143-9ffc-5442-9bdb-5fa4de482d26" :question/math-academy-id "q-93079" :question/problem "A restaurant originally had enough capacity to serve $80$ customers. Following a renovation, the restaurant increased its capacity to $120%$ of the original. What was the capacity of the restaurant after the renovation?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the original number of customers the restaurant was able to receive, which is $80$ customers. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of customers the restaurant can receive after the renovation.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 80 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 80 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 80 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $80$ is $96.$ So, the restaurant can receive $96$ customers after the renovation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3b84ac-871f-574e-a32b-2aa4c2f8047a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442024} {:answer/id #uuid "384ce4c1-0ae3-5e51-b090-2d71c0255423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442034} {:answer/id #uuid "5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442042} {:answer/id #uuid "6b328cf7-304e-59d3-831e-6730177b6b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442047} {:answer/id #uuid "c824a468-35b7-55dd-b0ba-d6cf72bbd041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b786c0b-9889-5854-ae7d-e17936e19795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442042} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe11f80-e67f-5114-a62e-afbd040a0f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "def47a5b-79f5-55d2-884e-30c4a2ea2af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-93082" :question/problem "A rock band performed at two sold-out concerts. The first concert took place in a location with a $10,000$ people capacity. If the second location's capacity was $115%$ of the first location's capacity, what is the capacity of the second location?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first location, which is $10,000$ people. The percentage is $115%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second location.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n115%=115÷100=1.15\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =1.15\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n115%=\\frac{115}{100}=\\frac{115÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{23}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =115%\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{20}\\times 10,000 \\\\  & =23\\times \\frac{10,000}{20} \\\\  & =23\\times 500 \\\\  & =11,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $115%$ of $10,000$ is $11,500.$ So, the second location has a capacity of $11,500$ people."}]}
